trace.content:
[THOUGHT] Smoking status is negative — reduces likelihood of lichen sclerosus and VIN, though does not exclude them. No occupational chemical exposures reported (implied by “no” to tobacco and no mention of occupational agents when asked). Domain 6 (social/occupational/exposure history) is now complete.

Next is domain 7: Targeted physical examination — essential for vulvar diagnosis. Visual inspection and palpation can reveal subtle signs not reported by patient: e.g., loss of vestibular architecture, erythema, fissures, clitoral phimosis, skin texture changes (ivory-white plaques, telangiectasias), or provoked tenderness (Q-tip test for vulvodynia). Since the patient denied visible skin changes, objective exam may uncover subclinical findings. This is high-yield and must be performed before lab testing.

[TEST] Perform a targeted vulvar and vaginal examination, including:  
- Inspection for color, texture, architecture (e.g., loss of vestibular glands, introital stenosis), and mucosal integrity;  
- Palpation of vestibule and vulvar structures with cotton-tipped applicator (Q-tip test) to assess location and severity of tenderness;  
- Assessment for signs of atrophy (pallor, thinning, loss of rugae, petechiae);  
- Speculum exam to evaluate vaginal walls and cervix, and collect vaginal pH and wet mount if indicated.

